1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,553
Every day, satellites measure the skin of the ocean:

2
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:06,957
its temperature, its height, its salt, the wind across it.

3
00:00:06,957 --> 00:00:09,188
What lies underneath, they cannot see.

4
00:00:09,188 --> 00:00:10,949
And that is where the heat is.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,213
This is Satelight.

6
00:00:14,213 --> 00:00:16,437
The ocean below, seen from space.

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:20,022
In the next six minutes:

8
00:00:20,022 --> 00:00:33,593
a dive to a thousand metres, how deep to fish, the fuel under a cyclone, this morning's ocean, the years before the floats, hidden heatwaves, the sonar layer, the floats that check it all, and what is not done yet.

9
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:37,086
INCOIS asks for one thing:

10
00:00:37,086 --> 00:00:42,698
estimate the three-dimensional ocean temperature, using only surface satellite observations.

11
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:47,650
So take one day in the Bay of Bengal, and go down.

12
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:51,996
Sunlight fades by 200 metres, and below it no satellite sees a thing.

13
00:00:51,996 --> 00:00:58,107
Satelight carries on, level by level, to a thousand metres, reconstructed from the surface alone.

14
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:04,030
It reads eight fields that satellites deliver every day:

15
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:10,398
sea surface temperature, salinity, sea level and its anomaly, and currents and winds, east and north.

16
00:01:10,398 --> 00:01:13,550
All on one grid, a quarter of a degree, every day.

17
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:19,416
An encoder compresses each cell's surface into 32 numbers:

18
00:01:19,416 --> 00:01:20,361
the embedding.

19
00:01:20,361 --> 00:01:27,720
A decoder turns them into temperature at the 15 standard depths, and masks every cell shallower than a level.

20
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:33,054
It learned from the GLORYS reanalysis, 2010 to 2020, and never takes GLORYS in.

21
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:35,652
For fishers.

22
00:01:35,652 --> 00:01:41,302
INCOIS already tells them where fish are likely to gather, along fourteen stretches of coast, every day.

23
00:01:41,302 --> 00:01:49,071
Satelight adds how deep the warm water goes at each of those zones, or that it reaches the sea floor, from the latest day the satellites allow.

24
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:51,960
பெரியமாங்கோடு:

25
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:57,037
சுமார் 115 மீட்டர் ஆழம் வரை வெதுவெதுப்பான நீர், கலப்பு அடுக்கு 20 மீட்டர்.

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,300
0.

27
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:14,003
2 metres too deep.

28
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,052
For forecasters:

29
00:02:17,052 --> 00:02:22,314
the fuel a cyclone draws on, the heat stored above 26 degrees, mapped every day.

30
00:02:22,314 --> 00:02:29,746
Follow Mocha up the Bay on the India Meteorological Department's own track, and watch the fuel drain in its wake.

31
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:33,394
0 kJ/cm².

32
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:33,815
9.

33
00:02:33,815 --> 00:02:44,968
In the Bay of Bengal, Satelight and GLORYS are level.

34
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,358
GLORYS arrives months after the fact.

35
00:02:49,358 --> 00:02:53,437
Satelight needs only what the satellites have already delivered.

36
00:02:53,437 --> 00:02:58,154
Press Now, and it shows the ocean of the twenty-fourth of September, 2026:

37
00:02:58,154 --> 00:02:59,492
days ago, not months.

38
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:02,519
Measured today:

39
00:03:02,519 --> 00:03:07,883
sea level arrives the same day, temperature a day late, salinity six days late.

40
00:03:07,883 --> 00:03:09,581
GLORYS is 99 days behind.

41
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:25,482
Scored against the real-time floats of those three months, with four fields it beats climatology from the surface to 150 metres, over the whole box and in the Bay; in the Arabian Sea, only to 75.

42
00:03:25,482 --> 00:03:29,543
With all eight, a month behind, to 200 metres in both basins.

43
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:33,016
It works backwards too.

44
00:03:33,016 --> 00:03:34,465
This is November 1997:

45
00:03:34,465 --> 00:03:41,252
the whole North Indian Ocean, reconstructed at every depth, from satellites that were already watching.

46
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,306
From 1993 to 2000, not one Argo float surfaced in this box.

47
00:03:47,306 --> 00:03:49,130
Satellites saw every day.

48
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:03,728
Scored on 2005 to 2009, years before its training, it beats climatology at every depth, in both basins, and stays behind GLORYS down to 500 metres.

49
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,151
Some heatwaves never reach the surface.

50
00:04:10,151 --> 00:04:15,500
A warm layer, fifty to a hundred and fifty metres down, under a sea that looks normal from space.

51
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:17,706
Magenta marks where Satelight finds one.

52
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:20,622
49.

53
00:04:20,622 --> 00:04:35,139
It over-flags, 484 false alarms, consistent with its warm thermocline.

54
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,596
And the sonar layer:

55
00:04:39,596 --> 00:04:48,552
the depth of the mixed layer, where a hull sonar's surface duct ends.

56
00:04:48,552 --> 00:04:51,926
This one is not there yet.

57
00:04:51,926 --> 00:04:52,186
5.

58
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,072
The headline scores are checked the same way:

59
00:04:57,072 --> 00:05:05,331
against Argo floats from 2023 and 2024, which the model never saw, each with its quality flag, data mode and source file.

60
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:17,545
Depth by depth, Satelight beats climatology from the surface to 300 metres, in both basins, and stays behind GLORYS, which had already assimilated these floats.

61
00:05:17,545 --> 00:05:19,617
The thermocline is the hard part:

62
00:05:19,617 --> 00:05:22,129
the error peaks there, and it runs warm.

63
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,481
The embedding is saved every day, as a file you can open.

64
00:05:28,481 --> 00:05:36,422
One encoder's embedding predicts the mixed-layer depth, never trained on, better than the raw inputs:

65
00:05:36,422 --> 00:05:36,737
255.

66
00:05:36,737 --> 00:05:40,118
No single embedding also sorts the seasons.

67
00:05:40,118 --> 00:05:41,926
That proof is half met.

68
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,480
Every number in this film comes from one harness, which anyone can run again from the repository.

69
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:55,273
What is not done yet:

70
00:05:55,273 --> 00:06:01,819
the embedding proof, a warm thermocline, the sonar layer, and translations that still need a native speaker.

71
00:06:01,819 --> 00:06:03,334
Each one is written down.

72
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:07,640
Satelight.

73
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:11,737
The code, the documents, and every number are in the repository.
